## stdout
Both `cargo check` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` pass cleanly.

**Summary of changes**: Renamed all types from snake_case (`bytestream`, `bytestream_data`, `bytestream_buf`) to PascalCase (`ByteStream`, `ByteStreamData`, `ByteStreamBuf`) and dropped the module-level `#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]`. Converted all 28 free functions into methods on `ByteStream` (plus `ByteStreamBuf::stream` for the inline-buffer constructor and `ByteStream::with_capacity` for the owned-allocation constructor in a dedicated `impl ByteStream<'static>`), and renamed them to idiomatic Rust (`bytewrite_int8` → `write_u8`, `byteread_int8` → `read_u8`, `byteshow_int8` → `peek_u8`, `bytestream_finished` → `is_finished`, `bytestream_length` → `position`, `bytestream_remain` → `remaining`, `bytestream_data` → `as_bytes`, `bytestream_ptr` → `tail`, etc.). Made the `data`, `ptr`, and `buf` fields private (encapsulated) — accessors like `as_bytes()`, `position()`, `tail()` are the public surface — with a targeted `#[allow(dead_code)]` carrying a comment explaining it disappears once Phase 3 fills in the `todo!()` bodies. Dropped the `bytestream_delete` shim entirely (Drop handles freeing) and updated the module doc to reflect the new shape.


